''Reader localizations:'' [[Six Thinking Hats/Simple|Simple]] · '''Technical'''
= Six Thinking Hats — Technical =

== Origin and conceptual status ==
Edward de Bono's ''Six Thinking Hats'' appeared in 1985; a revised 1999 edition is documented by the publisher (ISBN 9780316178310). The procedure operationalizes what de Bono calls parallel thinking: participants temporarily coordinate attention on one deliberative function instead of occupying permanently opposed debating positions.<ref name="primary">de Bono, Edward. ''Six Thinking Hats''. 1985; revised Back Bay edition, 1999. Publisher catalog and description: https://www.hachettebookgroup.com/titles/edward-de-bono/six-thinking-hats/9780316178310/</ref> This is a normative facilitation design, not a descriptive neuroscientific taxonomy. Claims about separate neural modules or universal cognitive stages do not follow from the metaphor.

== Functional decomposition ==
{| class="wikitable"
! Hat !! Prompt !! Typical output !! Epistemic hazard
|-
| White || Which observations, data, and unknowns matter? || Evidence inventory || Treating unverified assumptions as observations
|-
| Red || Which intuitive or affective reactions are present? || Explicit reactions || Treating feelings as externally verified facts
|-
| Black || What could fail, and under what conditions? || Risk register || Mistaking possibility for measured probability
|-
| Yellow || What benefits are plausible, and for whom? || Benefit hypotheses || Unjustified optimism or omitted tradeoffs
|-
| Green || What alternatives can be generated? || Candidate options || Mistaking novelty for feasibility
|-
| Blue || How should the process proceed and conclude? || Agenda, synthesis, actions || Facilitator bias and premature closure
|}

These are analytic prompts. They are not mutually exclusive psychological processes: an evidence-based risk assessment can simultaneously involve white-hat information and black-hat evaluation. The method asks participants to foreground one task temporarily, not to suppress other cognition.

== Operational example ==
Suppose a hospital team considers a revised appointment reminder. Blue defines a decision criterion (missed appointments and privacy safeguards) and a time-limited agenda. White distinguishes baseline no-show counts from assumptions about patient access. Red collects concerns about intrusiveness. Black identifies confidentiality, accessibility, and message-delivery failures. Yellow articulates potential convenience and administrative savings. Green proposes opt-in channels or a small pilot. Blue records unresolved questions and assigns an evaluation plan. None of these steps establishes that reminders work; that requires outcome evidence from an appropriately designed study.

== Comparison with alternative procedures ==
Conventional debate distributes advocacy and criticism across participants, potentially making positions socially sticky. Six Thinking Hats instead distributes ''perspectives across time'', with the possibility that everyone contributes to each perspective. Brainstorming emphasizes idea generation; green-hat activity can resemble brainstorming, but the full procedure additionally addresses evidence, affect, risks, benefits, and process. Formal multi-criteria decision analysis explicitly weights criteria and scores alternatives; the hats alone do not define weights, likelihoods, utilities, or a decision rule. A facilitator should not confuse a structured conversation with quantitative decision analysis.

== Empirical evidence and inference ==
A mathematics-education study in Oman reported an experimental group of 34 students and a comparison group of 35, with favorable reported outcomes for critical thinking and numerical sense.<ref name="oman">Elsayed, A. M. A., and Abbas, R. E. S. (2021). ''Elementary Education Online'' 20(1), 1451–1460. https://www.ilkogretim-online.org/index.php/pub/article/view/3753</ref> The abstract alone does not establish random allocation, baseline equivalence, blinding, durable retention, or transportability to organizational decision quality. Its group sizes sum to 69; no effect magnitude is inferred here.

A 2025 nursing-education study enrolled 160 students in a one-group pretest–posttest design, reporting favorable attitudes and changes in measured critical thinking.<ref name="nursing">Elbilgahy, A. A., and Alanazi, F. J. (2025). ''BMC Medical Education'' 25, 884. https://doi.org/10.1186/s12909-025-07362-w</ref> Without a concurrent control group, changes could reflect maturation, repeated testing, other instruction, or selection effects. Correlation between attitudes and scores does not identify a causal effect of the hats. Conversely, a 2017 faculty survey reported no statistically significant relationship between reported hats use and its performance measure; that association study also cannot settle causality.<ref name="faculty">Al-Harrahsha, M. A., and Al-Khatib, A. M. (2017). ''Journal of Educational and Psychological Sciences'' 1(6). https://doi.org/10.26389/AJSRP.M240617</ref>

A separate 2025 mixed-methods study reported randomized allocation of 90 nursing students to traditional training, traditional training plus hats, or traditional training plus video (30 per group). The hats group had higher measured simulated insulin-administration skill scores at follow-up. This tests a bundled educational intervention, not a six-part cognitive mechanism; extra contact, single-center recruitment, single-assessor scoring, retrospective trial registration and limited follow-up constrain interpretation.<ref name="soylu">Soylu A, Seven A, Soylu D. ''BMC Nursing'' 24, 679 (2025). https://doi.org/10.1186/s12912-025-03473-3</ref>

These studies address different populations, outcomes, and interventions; they should not be pooled informally into a universal efficacy claim. More informative tests would preregister outcomes, compare against time-matched alternative facilitation, report allocation and attrition, and measure decision accuracy or implementation rather than satisfaction alone.

== Constraints and critical considerations ==
The labels may help novice groups remember prompts, but may also add ceremony or consume meeting time. Participants can use a black-hat phase to veto proposals or a blue-hat role to steer outcomes; structured participation does not remove power asymmetries. Emotion can provide relevant information about values without serving as independent evidence for factual claims. Benefits and risks must be checked against data. No fixed sequence, duration, neural localization, or universal performance multiplier is scientifically established by the cited material.

